          "type": "turn",
          "question": "If someone has COPD and an exacerbation, is that one code or two?",
          "answer": "Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD) exacerbations are coded using the ICD-10 system. Specifically, COPD is classified under code J44. When a patient has an exacerbation of COPD, this can be reported alongside the COPD code, meaning that both conditions can be coded if documented.\n\nThe relevant passage states that \"where both are documented, both codes are reported.\" This means that if an exacerbation occurs in someone with underlying COPD, it is appropriate to use both the code for COPD (J44) and an additional code for the exacerbation. Therefore, the classification for COPD with exacerbation involves two codes: one for the COPD itself and another for the exacerbation (which would typically be represented by a specific code under the J44 category).\n\nFor reference, this information comes from the document on coding chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, specifically highlighting how exacerbations can be categorized alongside the disease (source: \"Coding chronic obstructive pulmonary disease: J44 and its two complication codes\").\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
